---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import Cta from '../components/home/Cta.astro';
import { SandboxSection } from '../components/demo/SandboxSection.tsx';
---
<Site
  title="Sandbox · Ouijit"
  description="Run coding agents with their permission checks off, held to their task's worktree by kernel-level limits, while the dev server beside them stays on the host.">
  <script is:inline>document.documentElement.classList.add('js');</script>

  <section class="hero">
    <h1 data-reveal>Permission checks off, <span class="hero-dim">machine intact.</span></h1>
    <p class="sandbox-lead" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay: 120ms">
      Every agent has a flag that stops the permission prompts. Most people end up using it, and then there is
      nothing between the agent and the rest of the machine. <strong>Ouijit draws the line at the task's
      worktree.</strong>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="home-sandbox">
    <SandboxSection client:visible />
    <div class="sandbox-prose" data-reveal>
      <h2>Already sandboxed?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Your agent's own sandbox.</strong> Claude Code's covers shell commands. Its file tools, MCP
        servers and hooks run outside it, it is off by default, and when it cannot start it runs the command
        unsandboxed. Codex's is stronger and Codex-only. Pi and OpenCode have none. Ouijit's holds the whole
        terminal, so everything the agent starts is inside, whichever agent it is.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>A devcontainer.</strong> A container is a second environment: a Dockerfile to keep current,
        rebuilds, tools to install again. Ouijit's sandbox is the kernel limiting a process on your machine.
        There is nothing to maintain and nothing to sync, and it opens as fast as any other terminal.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Cta />

  <script>
    import { initHomeChrome } from '../scripts/homeChrome';
    initHomeChrome();
  </script>
</Site>
